# Cases Reference Content

The foundation of Greek grammar: three roles a word can play, each marked by its ending.

## Purpose

Teach the case system conceptually before showing forms. Cases explain WHY word endings change. The learner meets the roles through plain-English handles (Doer, Target, Owner); the grammar term rides along as a badge.

## Key Insight

In English, word order shows meaning ("The dog bit the man" vs "The man bit the dog").
In Greek, word ENDINGS show meaning, so word order is flexible. These endings are called cases.

---

## Hero

<hero component="ReferenceHero" file="tabs/cases.tsx">
<title>One word, many roles.</title>
<thesis>Greek changes a word's ending to show what it's doing in a sentence: who's doing it, what it touches, who owns it.</thesis>
</hero>

---

## The Three Roles (TeachingCards)

<paradigm name="case-roles" purpose="Core case identification">
<principle>One TeachingCard per role: title is the learner handle, badge is the grammar term, the example is a MarkedGreek phrase. Only the marked words carry the case colour; the rest of the phrase stays neutral.</principle>

| Role (title) | Badge      | Description                                                  | Example (marked)                    | Translation            |
|--------------|------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|------------------------|
| Doer         | Nominative | who's doing the action, or what's being named after είναι    | ο καφές είναι ζεστός (ο καφές, ζεστός) | the coffee is hot   |
| Target       | Accusative | what the action touches, or after a preposition              | θέλω τον καφέ (τον καφέ)            | I want the coffee      |
| Owner        | Genitive   | whose something is                                           | η μυρωδιά του καφέ (του καφέ)       | the smell of the coffee |

</paradigm>

## Notes (ruled list under the cards)

<notes source="CASE_NOTES in components/cases-section.tsx">
- Word order is flexible: Ο σκύλος δάγκωσε τον άντρα and Τον άντρα δάγκωσε ο σκύλος both mean the dog bit the man (Doer and Target phrases coloured).
- Start with Doer and Target. Owner comes up with possession and a few formal prepositions.
- Every everyday preposition pulls Target: after σε, με, για, από, χωρίς there is no case decision.
- After είναι, both sides are the Doer: είναι and γίνομαι say what something is, so in η Χρυσάνθη είναι η μητέρα neither noun is a Target.
</notes>

---

## Look it up

<section name="look-it-up" heading="BandHeading">
<lede>Reading Greek, the article tells you the case. Writing Greek, the trigger word decides it.</lede>

1. `ArticleParadigm` (shared, from `components/case-table.tsx`): the article in case (rows) x gender (columns), singular and plural side by side. Case colour on row headers, gender colour on column chips, cells neutral. The Articles tab renders the same component.
2. "Trigger words that pull a case": one LookupCard per role that has triggers. `chip` = "{role} triggers" (e.g. "Target triggers"), `eyebrow` = the case name. Each trigger shows its pattern, meaning and MarkedGreek examples.

| Card            | Pattern                             | Meaning                                    | Examples                        |
|-----------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| Target triggers | After στο / στη / στον / σε         | going to, at somewhere                     | πηγαίνω στο σπίτι, στη δουλειά  |
| Target triggers | After με / από / για                | with, from, for                            | με τον φίλο, από το σπίτι       |
| Target triggers | Time expressions                    | when something happens                     | τη Δευτέρα, το πρωί             |
| Owner triggers  | μου / σου / του / της after a noun  | my, your, his, her: these words are already Owner forms | το σπίτι μου, η αδερφή της |
| Owner triggers  | After του / της + name              | belongs to someone                         | της Μαρίας, του Νίκου           |

Doer has no triggers, so it gets no card.
</section>

